NEWSMIN='https://www.thenewsminute.com/article/agr-verdict-airtel-vodafone-idea-and-tata-get-10-years-pay-dues-132043'
OUTBIZ='https://www.outlookbusiness.com/corporate/sunil-mittals-bharti-group-joins-french-govt-to-raise-155-bn-for-starlink-rival-eutelsat'
BTEUT='https://www.businesstoday.in/amp/latest/corporate/story/bharti-group-backed-eutelsat-eyes-strategic-lead-in-space-communications-with-eu135-billion-capital-raise-481218-2025-06-20'
TRIB='https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/india/election-commission-uploads-electoral-bonds-data-shared-by-sbi-on-its-website-600528'
MN2408='https://www.medianama.com/2024/08/223-urgent-need-satellite-spectrum-allocation-minimal-charges-airtel/'
MN2503='https://www.medianama.com/2025/03/223-what-we-know-dont-know-about-airtel-starlink-tie-up-india-satcom-telecom/'
add('sunil-mittal-1',
 'Reviewed on separate dated episodes for Sunil Bharti Mittal, chairman of Bharti Enterprises and Bharti Airtel and co-chairman of Eutelsat Group. (1) Satellite spectrum 2023–26: Airtel’s filed positions moved from opposing auctions (2023) to seeking level-playing-field treatment and lower charges for “traditional” rural, remote and government uses (2024), to a March 2025 Starlink distribution agreement and 2026 support for TRAI’s 2025 framework. Bharti also holds equity in Eutelsat, whose OneWeb arm is one of the three licensed operators the government says are at the same regulatory stage as Starlink. Musk’s October 2026 post names no one, and its follow-up names Ambani, not Mittal. (2) AGR: the Supreme Court’s rulings went against the telecom companies on the definition and gave ten years to pay; the September 2021 package applies to all operators. (3) Electoral bonds: press reports put Bharti-group purchases at Rs 198–246 crore under a scheme later struck down; the data do not link donors to recipients. None of these is an adjudicated exchange. No estimate is given.',
 'Company and group positions on spectrum pricing and sector relief; equity in a licensed satellite operator; lawful-at-the-time electoral-bond purchases; no adjudicated exchange of favours',
 '2021 (telecom package); 2023–2026 (satellite spectrum policy); 2019–2020 (AGR rulings); 2019–2024 (electoral bonds)',
 [r('Airtel told TRAI in October 2024 that satellite services serving urban and suburban retail customers raise a level-playing-field issue with terrestrial operators and that “traditional” uses (rural and remote areas, defence, government agencies, disaster recovery, cellular backhaul) could be priced differently from direct urban retail. At an August 2024 summit its officials asked for satellite spectrum usage charges of zero or about 1%. TechCrunch reported Mittal saying in October 2024 that satellite firms serving urban areas should “take the telecom licenses like everybody else”. These are documented advocacy positions aligned with Airtel’s terrestrial business; they are not evidence of how decisions were made.','Bharti Airtel comments to TRAI; MediaNama; TechCrunch',AIR24,'2024-10','Primary corporate lobbying position plus reported statement',PDF+' (Airtel comments filed in the consultation closing 26 Oct 2024; letter date not legible in the extracted text); MediaNama and TechCrunch reports read'),
  r('Press reports put Mittal-group electoral-bond purchases at Rs 246 crore across three companies (PTI via Tribune) and Bharti Airtel’s own at Rs 198 crore, eighth largest (Business Standard), from Election Commission data published in March 2024. The scheme was lawful when used and was struck down by the Supreme Court in February 2024 (the New Indian Express cites orders of 15 February and 11 March 2024); the published data do not show which party received which donor’s bonds. Donations alone do not justify points.','The Tribune (PTI); Business Standard; The New Indian Express',TRIB,'2024-03-14','Disclosure coverage (context only)','Page text reviewed; Election Commission files could not be opened (HTTP 406); the two totals differ by outlet and entity grouping'),
  r('Bharti Space is a major Eutelsat shareholder: Outlook Business reports a planned 18.70% post-transaction stake in the 2025 capital raise, and Mittal is quoted as Co-Chairman of Eutelsat Group. Eutelsat OneWeb is one of the three licensed GMPCS operators alongside Starlink and Jio. This is a personal and group commercial interest in a Starlink competitor, not evidence of preferential treatment.','Outlook Business; Business Today',OUTBIZ,'2025-06-19','Disclosed commercial interest','Page text reviewed (two outlets); final stake figures differ across later reports and were not checked against Eutelsat filings'),
  r('Mittal’s 15 September 2021 statement thanks the government, “under the decisive leadership of the Hon’ble Prime Minister”, for “seminal reforms”, and Airtel’s release says the package paves the way for a “three private plus one state owned” industry structure. This documents alignment and public gratitude over a sector-wide measure, not a personal favour.','Bharti Airtel press release',AIR21,'2021-09-15','Primary statement by subject; policy alignment'),
  r('Elon Musk’s 7 October 2026 post said “certain oligarchs” were blocking Starlink and “You can guess who they are”; CNBC notes Musk did not name anyone, and in the same article says Ambani’s Jio and Mittal’s Airtel together hold over 80% of the market; the link to Mittal is the press’s inference. CNBC said it had asked Airtel and Reliance for comment; no response was found.','CNBC',CNBC1008,'2026-10-08','Allegation by commercial rival; inference by the press, not a naming','Page text reviewed; the X post itself was not opened')],
 [r('The Supreme Court’s AGR rulings (October 2019, and 1 September 2020) went against the telecom companies on the definition and gave them ten years from 1 April 2021 to pay the balance in instalments, with interest, penalty and contempt for default. At that point Airtel was reported to owe Rs 25,976 crore after paying Rs 18,004 crore. Light Reading reports Airtel and Vodafone Idea had asked for 15 years. Adverse legal outcomes and a payment schedule are inconsistent with simple preferential treatment.','The News Minute; Light Reading',NEWSMIN,'2020-09-01','Court outcome reported','Page text reviewed (both outlets); the judgment text was not retrieved'),
  r('PIB’s 15 September 2021 release lists the package: non-telecom revenue excluded from AGR on a prospective basis, 100% automatic-route FDI, and a moratorium of up to four years on AGR and spectrum dues that the Cabinet approved “for all the Telecom Service Providers”. The measures are sector-wide and applied to Jio and Vodafone Idea as well as Airtel.','Press Information Bureau',PIB21,'2021-09-15','Primary government decision; sector-wide scope','Page text reviewed'),
  r('TRAI’s 9 May 2025 recommendations set the charge for NGSO fixed satellite services at 4% of AGR plus Rs 500 per urban subscriber (rural and remote exempt), justified by the incentive to reach rural areas and NGSO’s edge over GSO. The paper found a level-playing-field case between terrestrial services and NGSO FSS “not expected to arise”, rejecting the concern Airtel raised in 2024, and applies the same charge to every NGSO operator. DoT is reported to favour 5%; no final decision had been announced by 9 October 2026.','TRAI recommendations; MediaNama',TRAI25,'2025-05-09','Primary regulatory outcome that did not adopt the company’s level-playing-field argument',PDF+' (TRAI paras 2.35-2.39, 3.116-3.121); MediaNama for DoT’s reported 5% view'),
  r('Airtel was not an advocate of auction in 2023: its June 2023 TRAI counter-comments say auctioning satellite spectrum is “neither reasonable nor fair”. In May 2026 Airtel asked TRAI to apply its 2025 framework to the new network authorisation and only suggested feeder-link spectrum be assigned administratively under First Schedule entry 12, and said that if the Government permits direct-to-device service over satellite (MSS) spectrum, it should meet the same obligations as mobile operators. TechCrunch called Mittal a vocal advocate of the auction route; the Airtel filings reviewed from 2023, 2024 and 2026 do not ask for auctions.','Bharti Airtel comments to TRAI',AIR23,'2023-06-22','Primary evidence that the company position varied by use case and year','2023 counter-comments are a scanned PDF read by OCR; 2026 comments read as extracted text'),
  r('Airtel’s 11 March 2025 release announces an agreement with SpaceX to bring Starlink to its customers, “subject to SpaceX receiving its own authorizations”, in addition to its existing Eutelsat OneWeb alliance. The 8 October 2026 PIB release says all three licensees are at broadly the same stage, and Minister Scindia said on 9 October that each must separately clear security checks that rest with the Home Ministry. An Airtel partnership with Starlink and a shared regulatory stage cut against an Airtel veto.','Bharti Airtel press release; PIB; MediaNama',AIRSPX,'2025-03-11','Primary corporate announcement; official statement','Page text reviewed (Airtel, PIB); MediaNama report of the 9 October press conference')],
 'Sunil Mittal personally is distinct from Lakshmi Niwas Mittal (separate Forbes entry, individually bought Rs 35 crore of electoral bonds) and from his brothers Rajan and Rakesh. The sources mostly describe Bharti Airtel and Bharti Enterprises; Mittal is quoted directly only in the 2021 release, the 2025 Eutelsat release and one TechCrunch-reported remark. No rubric is added: no episode supports leverage, benefit and feedback together. Not verified: Airtel’s or Mittal’s reply to Musk (none found), the AGR judgment texts, the 2G and 2012 spectrum history (not reviewed; do not read the omission as clearance), the exact electoral-bond figures from Election Commission files, any personal political role, and Eutelsat’s final shareholding. The Medianama and TechCrunch reports are secondary. Airtel’s 2023 and 2024 positions on spectrum pricing are better read together than separately. Wealth, company size and donations alone do not support any point.')
ids={p['id'] for p in json.loads((ROOT/'data/billionaires.json').read_text())['people']}
assert set(P)<=ids
(ROOT/'data/review-batches/036.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(supersedes=['mukesh-ambani'],profiles=P,rubrics=R),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(len(P),'authored reviews;',len(R),'rubrics')
